{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c41b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a070a7-9081-7d73-bd3a-2f4da2eb7b1c"},"sequence":104,"recorded_at":1780531400000203,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"5d1923f0-6c67-41a3-89b5-230e3cfe396c","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"5d1923f0-6c67-41a3-89b5-230e3cfe396c","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"5d1923f0-6c67-41a3-89b5-230e3cfe396c"},"call_id":"call_01a070a7d1d67861bd25b498f3214210","text":"Read text file `internal/terminalbackend/refusal_arm_inventory_test.go`.\n1|// AST-derived refusal-arm inventory for the terminal backend packages.\n2|//\n3|// The failure this file exists to prevent: a brand-new production refusal\n4|// arm carrying a brand-new unasserted detail shipped while `gofmt`,\n5|// `go vet`, `go test ./...`, and `tracecheck` all stayed green. A\n6|// hand-maintained arm list cannot catch that; a derived one can, but only\n7|// if it is checked in BOTH directions. The forward direction alone passes\n8|// vacuously on a truncated derivation: every derived arm asserted is true\n9|// when the derivation silently dropped half the package. So this file\n10|// derives every refusal arm from the production AST, requires each to\n11|// resolve to exactly one declared row naming a real asserting test, and\n12|// requires every declared row to resolve to exactly one derived arm.\n13|//\n14|// Modelled on internal/canonicaljson/grammar_inventory_test.go: derivation\n15|// fails closed on an empty, short, or unparseable input, and the declared\n16|// table cannot move its own goalposts because the derivation never\n17|// consults it.\n18|//\n19|// Stated bounds (not inferred):\n20|//   - The inventory proves every arm is DECLARED with a resolving test.\n21|//     Execution and decision proof is each row's named test, measured by\n22|//     this package's suite. A row resolves textually (detail mention, or\n23|//     entry-plus-code/predicate mention); a wrongly attributed row that\n24|//     still resolves is a review finding, not a green light.\n25|//   - Attribution drift survives through that fallback: a\n26|//     dead-by-construction arm can resolve through a sibling site's test\n27|//     while pointing at the wrong guard. Live example: CheckTransition's\n28|//     \"operation vocabulary\" arm (conformance.go) is unreachable —\n29|//     ParseOperation admits exactly the ten operations transitionTable\n30|//     covers, so lookupTransition never misses — yet the row carries no\n31|//     bound and resolves through TestParseOperationAdmitsOnlyTheTenClosedOperations,\n32|//     which drives ParseOperation's own arm sharing the detail. The five\n33|//     re-parse arms are the contrast: they declare boundDefensiveReparse\n34|//     and TestDefensiveBoundsAreExactlyThese pins the set.\n35|//   - Kill inflation: both inventory directions fail on ANY added or\n36|//     deleted production arm by construction, so an arm-deleting mutant\n37|//     elsewhere reads as killed by the inventory alone with no behavioral\n38|//     change. Mutation scores measured with this file present are NOT\n39|//     comparable to pre-inventory scores; review the failing-test list,\n40|//     not just the exit code.\n41|//   - errors.New/fmt.Errorf sites are not wire refusals and carry no arm.\n42|//     They live only in validatePlatforms (funneled into one *Error arm\n43|//     whose detail set is pinned exactly) and DigestFile (I/O errors that\n44|//     must never be *Error). Both properties are asserted below, in every\n45|//     FuncDecl body plus every package-level var initializer, and any\n46|//     reference to either constructor outside direct-call position\n47|//     (e.g. `newPlain := errors.New`) fails the suite, so an aliased\n48|//     construction cannot ship unattributed.\n49|//   - The DigestFile half of that allowlist is function-wide with no\n50|//     detail pin, unlike validatePlatforms' derived exact set: a new\n51|//     errors.New on any path inside DigestFile passes the gate (and the\n52|//     never-wire behavioral assertion, which only drives the current\n53|//     paths). Disclosed, not closed.\n54|//   - Custom error types outside the two recognised spellings (*Error,\n55|//     errors.New, fmt.Errorf) are outside both derivations: a bespoke\n56|//     error type smuggled as a refusal survives. Disclosed, not closed.\n57|//   - Constructor Details are single static literals by assertion: the\n58|//     derivation fatals on any mismatchf/integrityFailure call without\n59|//     exactly one string-literal argument, so format-verb interpolation\n60|//     cannot enter the table. Zero multi-argument sites today; go vet's\n61|//     printf analyzer independently refuses a non-constant format string.\n62|package terminalbackend\n63|\n64|import (\n65|\t\"errors\"\n66|\t\"go/ast\"\n67|\t\"go/parser\"\n68|\t\"go/token\"\n69|\t\"os\"\n70|\t\"path/filepath\"\n71|\t\"strconv\"\n72|\t\"strings\"\n73|\t\"testing\"\n74|)\n75|\n76|// refusalArm is one derived production refusal: a wire-code *Error\n77|// construction site keyed by file, enclosing function (receiver-qualified\n78|// for methods), code symbol, static detail, and occurrence index within\n79|// that key. The index keeps two identical arms in one function distinct:\n80|// adding a second arm with an already-declared key fails on the missing\n81|// #2 row instead of hiding behind #1.\n82|type refusalArm struct {\n83|\tfile       string\n84|\tfunction   string\n85|\tcode       string\n86|\tdetail     string\n87|\toccurrence int\n88|}\n89|\n90|// constructorFunctions build refusals instead of issuing them. Their own\n91|// *Error sites are the funnel, not arms.\n92|var constructorFunctions = map[string]bool{\n93|\t\"mismatchf\":        true,\n94|\t\"integrityFailure\": true,\n95|}\n96|\n97|// passthroughDetail marks the single dynamic-detail funnel: Registration\n98|// validation wraps validatePlatforms errors via err.Error(). Every other\n99|// Detail expression must be a static string literal; a new dynamic detail\n100|// fails the derivation rather than entering the table silently.\n101|const passthroughDetail = \"passthrough:err.Error()\"\n102|\n103|// deriveRefusalArms walks every production file of this package (every\n104|// *.go except *_test.go, so a new production file is scanned without\n105|// anyone remembering it) and returns every refusal arm in source order.\n106|// It fails closed: an unparseable file, a non-static code or detail, and\n107|// an empty result are all fatal, never an empty table.\n108|//\n109|// Coverage is declaration-complete, not FuncDecl-complete: every FuncDecl\n110|// body (including nested func literals, methods, and init) plus every\n111|// package-level var initializer is scanned, so a refusal arm hiding in a\n112|// package-level `var f = func() *Error {...}` is derived like any other.\n113|// Constructor references outside direct-call position (`refuse :=\n114|// mismatchf`) are rejected outright by rejectConstructorAliases below:\n115|// an aliased refusal cannot be inventoried, so it fails the suite rather\n116|// than shipping silently.\n117|func deriveRefusalArms(t *testing.T) []refusalArm {\n118|\tt.Helper()\n119|\n120|\tmatches, err := filepath.Glob(\"*.go\")\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c41d","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a070a7-9081-7d73-bd3a-2f4da2eb7b1c"},"sequence":105,"recorded_at":1780531400000205,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"5d1923f0-6c67-41a3-89b5-230e3cfe396c","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"5d1923f0-6c67-41a3-89b5-230e3cfe396c","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"5d1923f0-6c67-41a3-89b5-230e3cfe396c"},"call_id":"call_01a070a7d27a7890b46ba5d4e771053a","text":"Read text file `internal/terminalbackend/terminalbackend.go`.\n1|// Package terminalbackend implements the TerminalBackend registry: canonical\n2|// backend identities, implementation/protocol version tuples, external\n3|// executable trust admission, and duplicate or drift refusal.\n4|//\n5|// Normative scope is relux-works/agent-session-manager-spec@v0.5.0 §4.B\n6|// (registry, identity, discovery, trust), §4.1 (backend interface versions),\n7|// §6.5 (Configuration 3.0.0 TerminalBackend extension), and §7.A (Provider\n8|// Protocol 3.0.0 Terminal Instance binding). Manifest/Probe schemas,\n9|// capability evidence, and generation-bound admission are defined in\n10|// manifest.go; lifecycle operations and legacy v0.4.3 translation belong to\n11|// the sibling story tasks and are deliberately not defined here.\n12|package terminalbackend\n13|\n14|import (\n15|\t\"crypto/sha256\"\n16|\t\"encoding/hex\"\n17|\t\"errors\"\n18|\t\"fmt\"\n19|\t\"os\"\n20|\t\"path/filepath\"\n21|\t\"regexp\"\n22|\t\"sort\"\n23|\t\"strings\"\n24|\t\"sync\"\n25|\t\"unicode/utf8\"\n26|\n27|\t\"github.com/relux-works/agent-session-manager/internal/scalar\"\n28|)\n29|\n30|// Wire error codes are the exact Section 15.3 codes the registry may report.\n31|// They are a subset of the pinned catalog error vocabulary; no other code is\n32|// emitted by this package.\n33|const (\n34|\t// CodeNotFound reports an unknown or malformed backend identity. A failed\n35|\t// read is never absence: callers must not treat this as \"no backend\".\n36|\tCodeNotFound = \"terminal_backend_not_found\"\n37|\t// CodeAmbiguous reports a duplicate or ambiguous backend ID. Re-registering\n38|\t// an identical record is still refused: identity registration is not\n39|\t// idempotent.\n40|\tCodeAmbiguous = \"terminal_backend_ambiguous\"\n41|\t// CodeUntrusted reports a disabled, unresolvable, or digest-mismatched\n42|\t// external executable. Trust is established before any probe.\n43|\tCodeUntrusted = \"terminal_backend_untrusted\"\n44|\t// CodeDrift reports a re-registration whose version tuple, kind,\n45|\t// platforms, or executable digest differs from the admitted record.\n46|\tCodeDrift = \"terminal_backend_implementation_drift\"\n47|\t// CodeRestoreMismatch reports an attempt to substitute the configured\n48|\t// default for the exact prior binding on restore or resume.\n49|\tCodeRestoreMismatch = \"terminal_backend_restore_mismatch\"\n50|\t// CodeStaleGeneration reports a generation that is out of bound or does\n51|\t// not match the validated binding.\n52|\tCodeStaleGeneration = \"terminal_backend_stale_generation\"\n53|)\n54|\n55|// Canonical built-in backend identities. The ax. namespace is reserved (§4.B);\n56|// no third-party registration may use it.\n57|const (\n58|\tBuiltinTmux   = \"ax.tmux\"\n59|\tBuiltinConpty = \"ax.conpty\"\n60|)\n61|\n62|// reservedNamespace is the only namespace this implementation may mint.\n63|const reservedNamespace = \"ax.\"\n64|\n65|// maxIDBytes is the Section 4.B bound: 1-128 ASCII bytes.\n66|const maxIDBytes = 128\n67|\n68|// maxGenerationRunes is the Section 4.B/7.A bound: string[1..256] counts\n69|// UTF-8 characters (SPEC.md:321), not bytes; terminal_backend_id is the\n70|// only member of this package still bounded in ASCII bytes.\n71|const maxGenerationRunes = 256\n72|\n73|// maxProtocolVersions is the Section 4.B bound: sorted unique semver[1..32].\n74|const maxProtocolVersions = 32\n75|\n76|var (\n77|\tidPattern = regexp.MustCompile(`^[a-z][a-z0-9]*(?:[.-][a-z0-9]+)*$`)\n78|\t// semverPattern implements Semantic Versioning 2.0.0 in full, matching the\n79|\t// grammar the canonical and config packages enforce for the same members.\n80|\tsemverPattern = regexp.MustCompile(`^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)\\.(0|[1-9][0-9]*)(?:-(?:0|[1-9][0-9]*|[0-9]*[A-Za-z-][0-9A-Za-z-]*)(?:\\.(?:0|[1-9][0-9]*|[0-9]*[A-Za-z-][0-9A-Za-z-]*))*)?(?:\\+[0-9A-Za-z-]+(?:\\.[0-9A-Za-z-]+)*)?$`)\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
